+
+      2. If you open a file and need to get to an area around a specific line that is not in the first 100 lines, say line 583, don't just use the scroll_down command multiple times. Instead, use the goto 583 command. It's much quicker. It is okay to scroll if you don't know the exact line number. Similarly when searching for a function, instead of scrolling down, use the search command to find the function name.
+
+      3. If a patch is successfully applied to some files, you should stage them using `git add` before manually applying the patch to other files. This will help you to keep track of the changes you've made.
+
+      4. Always make sure to look at the currently open file and the current working directory (which appears right after the currently open file). The currently open file might be in a different directory than the working directory! Note that some commands, such as 'create', open files, so they might change the current open file.
+
+      5. When editing files, it is easy to accidentally to write code with incorrect indentation or make other mistakes. Always check the code after you issue an edit to make sure that it reflects what you wanted to accomplish. If it didn't, issue another command to fix it. ALWAYS USE PROPER INDENTATION WHEN EDITING FILES EVEN IF THE SYNTAX IS CORRECT WITHOUT INDENTATION. Code readability is important.
+
+      6. When editing files, first explain the code you want to edit and why it is causing the problem. Then explain the edit you want to make and how it fixes the problem. Explain how the edit does not break existing functionality.
+
+      7. Do not try to install any packages with `pip`, `conda`, or any other way. This will usually not work. If the environment is not set up correctly, try to fix the issue without executing python code or running any tests that require the package installed.
+
+      8. Preserve the comments from the original patch whenever possible. They are important for understanding the code.
+
+      9. Use the same commit message as the original patch. If the commit message is not relevant to the target branch, you can change it to a more relevant one. ALWAYS COMMIT THE CHANGES AND GENERATE THE PATCH USING "git format-patch -1". And then rename it to desired name.
+
+      10. Don't be over confident. Open the patch file and CVE description file multiple times to make sure you're doing the right thing. If you're not sure about the fix, don't hesitate to ask for reading again or searching the Internet.
+
+      STRATEGY:
+
+      1. Always start by verifying the environment. Make sure the `/backport` directory exists. If not, create it. Confirm that you are inside the correct repo and checkedout the correct branch or tag. Make sure your email address and the name are set correctly in the git configuration.
+
+      2. Use the `browse` tool by `browse "get description from the Internet preferrable from https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/<CVE-ID>"`. Do this to make-sure that the fix is relevant to the CVE.
+
+      3. First try to cherrypick the commit or directly apply the patch to the target branch. If the patch applies cleanly, you can submit it. If the patch partially applies, you can try to apply the patch partially by using `--exclude` option for the git command to exclude failing files and partially applying the patch to other files. After applying partially, make sure that you staged the changes. __always__ make sure you apply the patch to the files that are not failing.
+
+      4. If there are any documentaion or test code chainges in the patch, remove it from the patch if they're not applying cleanly. Don't struggle with those changes. Files with rst, md and similar extensions are document files. Also, the unit test codes are not being shipped in the final product. So, you can ignore them.
+
+      5. If cherry-pick or git apply fails on documentation or test code, you can try reapplying the patch while ignoring those documentation and test changes. Use `git apply <original_patch> --exclude=<doc_file_1> --exclude=<doc_file_2> --exclude=<test_file_1> --exclude=<text_file_2> ...` to apply the patch. If the patch applies cleanly, you can submit it.
+
+      6. You don't need to worry about testing the fix. Try minimal self testing if available. But if it is not available or not running properl, don't worry about it.
+
+      7. When you need to manually apply the changes, read the changes yet to be applied from the original patch caefully. The `open` command only shows 100 lines, if the patch is longer make sure you scroll down to see the complete file. Make sure you understand all the chunks of the patch that need to be manually applied. Print all the description of changes it does in your thoughts as it will be served to you in the history in future prompts. And start making changes one by one.
+
+      8. When you're manually applying a patch, make sure that you apply all the chunks from original patch are considered. If you are not ignoring a chunk of the patch, you should give reason why you are ignoring it.
+
+      9. Locate relevant code using the find and search commands. `open` the file you want to edit. Use these tools most of the time to make yourself familier with the code base. Better understanding is preferred.
+
+      9. Use the `edit` command to perform edits.
+
+      10. When you think you've fixed the bug, commit the changes and create the final patch into /backport/<cve_id>_<package>.patch. If the directory /backport doesn't exist, please create it. But don't include the backport directory in the patch.
+
+      11. Even though original patch has the CVE information, ALWAYS use the `browse` tool by asking it to `get description from the Internet preferrable from https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/<CVE-ID>`. You can call `browse "get description from the Internet preferrable from https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/<CVE-ID>"`. Do this to make-sure that the fix is relevant to the CVE.
+
+
+      (Open file: {{open_file}})
+      (Current directory: {{working_dir}})
+      bash-$
